, node3 are available from the other members (three-member Galera multi-master cluster)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Cluster formed with node1 bootstrap (galera_new_cluster) then node2 and node3 sequentially joined via IST, reaching cluster_size=3/Synced on all three (cmd-5294eae5f4714fff, cmd-f7f25b5d07194461, cmd-41025d9396194167, cmd-baa2d07451644031, cmd-6aecf4f43579455e, cmd-72a4380503f247ec). Writes on node1 were read back on node2 and node3 (cmd-b80e5dc884c34bae/cmd-31c747534f624afa/cmd-c85f0c5c0e014753), a write on node3 was read back on node1 and node2 (cmd-a327e2595bb4432a/cmd-1c9d84f7a7a845bc), and a later write on node2 was read back on node1 and node3 (cmd-749a179f2b9a4179/cmd-1f444f88a7a44787/cmd-15a09d95510f4749). All three nodes were confirmed both as write source and as replication target.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5294eae5f4714fff\",\"cmd-f7f25b5d07194461\",\"cmd-41025d9396194167\",\"cmd-b80e5dc884c34bae\",\"cmd-31c747534f624afa\",\"cmd-c85f0c5c0e014753\",\"cmd-a327e2595bb4432a\",\"cmd-1c9d84f7a7a845bc\",\"cmd-749a179f2b9a4179\",\"cmd-1f444f88a7a44787\",\"cmd-15a09d95510f4749\"]},{\"id\":\"availability-during-outage\",\"requirement\":\"Database stays available (readable and writable) when one member is unavailable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3 was stopped (cmd-0ff8a99f0e8444c3); node1 immediately showed wsrep_cluster_status=Primary at reduced size=2 and successfully accepted a new write (cmd-90474162dc5743f9); node2 confirmed it saw the same write, showing the two-node quorum stayed live and consistent (cmd-c1a1314d08e040da). This directly demonstrates the healthy baseline, the bounded disruption, and continued availability during it.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0ff8a99f0e8444c3\",\"cmd-90474162dc5743f9\",\"cmd-c1a1314d08e040da\"]},{\"id\":\"auto-resync\",\"requirement\":\"An unavailable member resynchronizes automatically when it returns\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3 was restarted after being stopped for a period during which node1 had accepted a write; upon restart it performed IST automatically and rejoined at cluster_size=3, wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced, and its local copy of galeratest.t included the row written while it was down (cmd-ad01eb0261ca4b8c) — a direct before/during/after transition covering the full disruption-and-recovery cycle.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0ff8a99f0e8444c3\",\"cmd-90474162dc5743f9\",\"cmd-ad01eb0261ca4b8c\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Provisioning steps (hosts entries, package installs, firewall ports, galera.cnf, bootstrap, enable-on-boot) each map clearly to the task or the mandatory restart protocol, and package/service health remained clean after the run (no failed units, dnf healthy, per global:after_executor:*:failed-services and :package-health). One unnecessary/risky transient mutation occurred: cmd-9e34997ab04e48c1 attempted to set the root password via a missing 'openssl' binary; the trailing ALTER USER still executed with an effectively empty password (confirmed by the subsequent passwordless root login in cmd-bde6d2de9e804d83), briefly leaving the root account with a weak/empty credential. This was corrected within seconds by cmd-7925aacfdaac4fe1, which regenerated a proper random password via python3 and re-ran ALTER USER; no evidence this weakened state persisted. cmd-990de5a0299f4688 was a read-only check of the resulting secret file. cmd-6853c0a00e5a442e (node2's first mariadb start attempt) failed with an auth error due to a missing password variable in that single invocation, not a state mutation. The restart-protocol reboots (cmd-2ab067cd859b4c7c/c03059a19c844128/d1c7eeeec9f447d7) are required by the independent protocol and each node's mariadb/cluster state was fully restored before proceeding to the next node, with pre-restart boot identity and healthy baseline, reboot acknowledgement, changed post-restart boot identity, and recovered service/data/cluster state shown for every node, cross-checked against the fixed after-prepare and after-executor boot-identity snapshots (matching values for all three nodes).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9e34997ab04e48c1\",\"cmd-bde6d2de9e804d83\",\"cmd-7925aacfdaac4fe1\",\"cmd-990de5a0299f4688\",\"cmd-6853c0a00e5a442e\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor successfully stood up a genuine three-member MariaDB Galera cluster on node1/node2/node3, with direct evidence of bidirectional (multi-master) replication from all three nodes, continued availability and writability with one node down, and automatic IST-based resynchronization of a stopped node upon return, including verification that data written during the outage was caught up. The independently required node-restart evidence protocol was also completed for all three nodes with clear pre/post boot-identity change, reboot acknowledgement, and full cluster/data recovery verification, consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. One transient, self-corrected mutation (an accidental empty root password from a failed openssl call) is the only notable hygiene issue; it left no lasting residue and was fixed within the same short sequence.\",\"limitations\":[\"The transient empty-root-password window (between cmd-9e34997ab04e48c1 and cmd-7925aacfdaac4fe1) was inferred from command outputs rather than directly asserted by the executor; its exact duration and any exposure risk cannot be fully confirmed from the captured evidence.\",\"Multi-master write-then-read-elsewhere evidence for node2 as the originating writer only appears in the post-restart-cycle check (cmd-749a179f2b9a4179), not in the initial pre-disruption verification phase, though this is s